<interrogationes unitas="fons">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a Unicode code point into a glyph index for the 6x8 font?
    <responsum symbolum="fons_codepoint_ad_glypham utf8_decodere tabula_pixelorum_pingere_chordam">Decode
      the text with utf8_decodere and pass each code point to
      fons_codepoint_ad_glypham: ASCII and drawn Latin-1 slots map to
      themselves (U+00AD to its deliberately blank slot), U+00A0
      becomes a space, curly and low-9 quotes, dashes, the ellipsis and
      the minus sign become their ASCII look-alikes, the bullet
      becomes slot 0x07, and Greek capitals that look Latin (and small
      omicron) become the Latin letter.
      Everything else, negative input included, is FONS_TOFU (0x7F).
      tabula_pixelorum_pingere_chordam does all of this for a whole
      chorda.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I tell in advance whether a character will draw as tofu?
    <responsum symbolum="fons_est_mappatum fons_codepoint_ad_glypham fons_8x16_codepoint_ad_glypham">For
      the 6x8 font, yes: fons_est_mappatum is true when the lookup is
      not FONS_TOFU. Because tofu is slot 0x7F, U+007F itself also
      reports as unmapped. There is no such predicate for the 8x16
      font; compare fons_8x16_codepoint_ad_glypham with 0x7F
      yourself (U+2302 legitimately maps there too).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are FONS_PARVUS and FONS_TITULI, and how big is a glyph?
    <responsum symbolum="fons_latitudo fons_altitudo Mandatum tabula_pixelorum_pingere_chordam_fonte">The
      two font ids a Mandatum.fons field can hold: FONS_PARVUS (0, the
      6x8 HP 100LX + Alcuin face, the default) and FONS_TITULI (1, the
      8x16 Nix8810 header face in CP437 order).
      fons_latitudo and fons_altitudo give the glyph cell in pixels
      (6x8 or 8x16); any unknown id is treated as FONS_PARVUS.
      Every glyph has the same advance, tofu included.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the 8x16 font need its own code point lookup instead of reusing the 6x8 one?
    <responsum symbolum="fons_8x16_codepoint_ad_glypham fons_codepoint_ad_glypham" via="lib/fons.worklog.md">The
      8x16 glyphs stay in native CP437 order, while the 6x8 high slots
      are a hybrid (Latin-1 at its own code points). So the 8x16
      lookup inverts the CP437 tables (0x80-0xFF and the 0x01-0x1F
      dingbats), maps Latin-1 letters CP437 lacks to their base letter
      (A grave becomes A), applies the same quote and dash
      substitutions, and returns 0x7F for anything else.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can the small font draw Greek or other non-Latin scripts?
    <responsum symbolum="fons_codepoint_ad_glypham fons_8x16_codepoint_ad_glypham" via="include/glyphae_ductae.h">Only
      the Greek letters that look Latin (Alpha, Beta, Epsilon, ... and
      small omicron) are mapped, to the Latin letter; alpha, pi, Gamma
      and the rest are tofu in the 6x8 font. The 8x16 font also has
      the Greek letters CP437 carries (alpha, Gamma, pi, Sigma, ...).
      The terminal draws other symbols itself through
      glyphae_ductae.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
